ML_PAGE("l2-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 2 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain, apply to the scenario, and justify. The model answers below show the standard to aim for; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l2\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The Lecture 2 reasoning pattern</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Problem</b><p>What is the business objective, and what ML task does it become?</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Data issue</b><p>Which variable types? What is wrong - missing, duplicates, wrong types, outliers?</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Technique + why</b><p>Name the fix and justify it for this data.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Consequence + consistency</b><p>State the effect, and that preprocessing is learned on training data only.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Choice + reason + effect is what subjective marking rewards.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Use median.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">A technique name with no reason and no consequence.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"The missing income values can be imputed rather than deleting the observations, particularly if retaining the rows matters. Since the salary distribution contains extreme values, the median is preferable to the mean because it is less influenced by outliers. This gives a more representative replacement while preserving the rest of each customer record.\"</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Differentiate CRISP-DM from the ML pipeline.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l2-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l2-1\">CRISP-DM is a project framework with a broad business focus: it guides the whole team from business understanding through data understanding, preparation, modelling and evaluation to deployment. The ML pipeline is the technical workflow inside it - the code that retrieves data, prepares it (wrangling, feature engineering, scaling), trains, evaluates and deploys the model. CRISP-DM decides why and what; the pipeline executes how.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain preprocessing consistency and data leakage.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l2-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l2-2\">Preprocessing parameters - scaling min/max or mean/SD, category mappings - must be learned from training data and applied unchanged to test and future data. If they are recalculated using test data, information from the test set leaks into training and accuracy is falsely inflated. For example, scaling with a maximum of 100 taken from the test set lets future data shape the model. The correct order is split first, fit on train, transform both.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Why can label encoding be inappropriate, and what is the alternative?</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l2-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l2-3\">Label encoding assigns integers (Red = 0, Blue = 1, Green = 2), so a model may infer Green &gt; Red or a distance of 2 between them - relationships that don't exist for nominal data. One-hot encoding creates a binary column per category, avoiding implied order. Label/ordinal coding is appropriate only when order is genuine, such as Low &lt; Medium &lt; High.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Why does feature scaling matter? Compare min-max and standardization.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l2-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l2-4\">Features in large units (income 45,000) dominate those in small units (age 25) in distance-based algorithms such as KNN or SVM, regardless of real importance. Min-max scaling compresses values to 0-1 using (x - min)/(max - min); standardization centres values at mean 0 with standard deviation 1 using (x - μ)/σ. Both keep the ordering; they change the ruler, not the information.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">JUSTIFY</span>\n        <h4>Should duplicate records always be deleted?</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l2-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l2-5\">No. Duplicates should be checked first with <code>df.duplicated()</code>. Accidental copies distort counts and means and, if they appear in both training and test sets, leak information. But identical-looking records may be genuine repeated events - two customers buying the same coffee at the same time - so they should be removed only when they are confirmed errors.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">SCENARIO</span>\n        <h4>Telecom churn dataset: outline the preparation.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l2-6\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l2-6\">The goal is reducing churn, so the task is supervised classification (Churn yes/no). Monthly Charges has missing values: investigate why, then impute (median if outliers exist). Contract Type is categorical: encode rather than feeding strings. Convert Signup Date to datetime and derive tenure. Scale numeric features if the algorithm is scale-sensitive. Learn every step on training data and reuse it for test and future customers.</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Practice questions and worked answers from the notes</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P1</span><span>How to answer Lecture 2 subjective questions</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>For most scenario questions, use this reasoning pattern:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Identify Problem → Identify Data Issue → Choose Technique → Explain Why → Explain Consequence\"><span>Identify Problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Identify Data Issue</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Choose Technique</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Explain Why</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Explain Consequence</span></div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A customer dataset contains some missing income values and several extreme salaries. Recommend an appropriate way to handle the missing income.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use median.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A stronger answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The missing income values can be imputed rather than deleting the entire observations, particularly if retaining the rows is important. Since the salary distribution contains extreme values, the median is preferable to the mean because the median is less influenced by outliers. This provides a more representative replacement value while preserving the remaining information in those customer records.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture itself distinguishes mean imputation for roughly normal numerical data from median imputation when outliers are present.</p>\n<p>Notice the pattern:</p>\n<p><strong>choice + reason + effect.</strong></p>\n<p>That is what subjective marking rewards.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P2</span><span>How to answer a missing-data scenario</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A temperature sensor records a value every minute. One minute is missing, but temperature usually changes gradually. Recommend a method.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Forward filling may be appropriate because this is sequential time-series data and the immediately previous sensor state is likely to remain reasonably representative for a short missing interval. This preserves the record without deleting the observation. However, this approach relies on the assumption that the underlying state changes gradually; it would be less appropriate if measurements can change abruptly.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That last sentence adds evaluation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P3</span><span>Likely subjective encoding question</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>Explain why one-hot encoding may be preferable to directly assigning integers to a categorical variable such as colour.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Directly assigning integers such as Red = 0, Blue = 1 and Green = 2 converts the categories into numbers but may cause an algorithm to interpret an artificial ordering or magnitude between them. One-hot encoding instead represents each category using its own binary feature, such as Red = [1,0,0], so no category is treated as numerically greater than another. Therefore, one-hot encoding better preserves the unordered nature of such categorical data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P4</span><span>A strong scaling answer</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>Feature scaling is particularly important for algorithms whose calculations depend on numerical distance. For example, if Age ranges from 20 to 70 while Income ranges from RM20,000 to RM500,000, Income may dominate the distance calculation simply due to its larger numerical magnitude. Scaling places features on more comparable mathematical scales. The lecture introduces Min-Max scaling, which compresses values between 0 and 1, and standardization, which centers a variable around mean 0 with standard deviation 1.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's a solid 5-mark response.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P5</span><span>Likely <code>groupby()</code> subjective question</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>Explain the output of:</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Good answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The statement divides the transaction records according to <code>user_type</code>, calculates the mean value of <code>price</code> separately for each user group, and combines those averages into a summarized result. This follows the split-apply-combine process: split records by user category, apply the mean operation, and combine the group-level results.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Much better than:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It groups data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P6</span><span>Full scenario example</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Suppose the subjective question says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A telecom company wants to predict customer purchasing behaviour. Its dataset contains Age, Income, Plan Type and Sign-Up Date. Some Income values are missing, Plan Type is text, and Sign-Up Date has been loaded as a string. Explain how you would prepare the data before modelling.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Reason through it sequentially.</p>\n<h5>First: inspect</h5>\n<p>Use tools such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.info()\ndf.describe()\n</code></pre>\n<p>to identify:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">types\nmissingness\nnumerical ranges\npossible unusual observations\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Second: Income</h5>\n<p>Investigate the distribution and missingness.</p>\n<p>If only a few rows are missing and the dataset is huge, removal may be acceptable.</p>\n<p>Otherwise impute.</p>\n<p>If there are serious outliers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Median}$$</div>\n<p>may be preferable.</p>\n<h5>Third: Plan Type</h5>\n<p>It's categorical.</p>\n<p>If categories have no meaningful numeric ordering, encode them in a way that avoids creating false magnitude, such as one-hot representation.</p>\n<h5>Fourth: Sign-Up Date</h5>\n<p>Convert it to a proper datetime type, then relevant components can be extracted if needed.</p>\n<h5>Fifth: Scaling</h5>\n<p>If the selected model is sensitive to feature magnitudes, scale numerical variables such as Age and Income using parameters learned from the training data.</p>\n<h5>Sixth: avoid leakage</h5>\n<p>Do not use test-set information to fit the scaler or other preprocessing transformations.</p>\n<p>That is a very respectable subjective answer because you've shown a <strong>workflow</strong>, not thrown disconnected vocabulary at the examiner.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P7</span><span>Very likely question: explain preprocessing consistency and leakage</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A good 8-10 mark answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Preprocessing consistency means that new or test observations must undergo the same transformations used during model training. For example, if numerical scaling parameters or categorical mappings are learned from the training data, those same parameters should be applied to test and deployment data rather than recalculated independently. This ensures that the model receives features represented in the same way as during training. It also prevents data leakage. Leakage occurs when information from the test set influences model development or preprocessing, such as using test values when calculating scaling minimums and maximums. This makes evaluation unreliable because the supposedly unseen test data has already influenced the model-building process. Therefore, preprocessing rules should be learned from training data and then reused unchanged for test and future observations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I would learn that paragraph's <strong>logic</strong>, not memorize every word.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P8</span><span>Very likely question: explain why data preparation matters</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Model answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Data preparation is necessary because real-world datasets may contain missing values, duplicates, inconsistent formats, incorrect data types, categorical text and features measured on very different scales. These issues can prevent algorithms from operating correctly or can distort the patterns that the model learns. Data preparation therefore involves inspecting the dataset, cleaning errors, handling missing information, converting data types, encoding categories, scaling relevant numerical features and deriving useful features. The purpose is to transform messy raw data into a reliable and consistent representation suitable for modelling. As emphasized in the lecture, algorithms cannot compensate for fundamentally poor input data; therefore, data preparation directly affects downstream model quality.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P9</span><span>Very likely question: missing data</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>If asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Discuss methods for handling missing data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Your answer should compare, not merely list.</p>\n<p>You could write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Missing values can be handled through deletion or imputation depending on the amount and nature of the missing data. <code>dropna()</code> removes affected records and may be reasonable when the dataset is large and only a very small proportion of values is missing. However, excessive deletion can unnecessarily reduce the dataset. Alternatively, numerical values can be imputed using a central value; the lecture recommends mean for suitable normally distributed data and median where outliers are important. For ordered or time-series data, forward or backward filling can also be used when neighbouring observations provide a reasonable estimate, such as when the previous state tends to persist. Therefore, the method should be selected according to the data's distribution and structure rather than applying one technique blindly.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's the level of comparison you want.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P10</span><span>Very likely question: categorical encoding and scaling</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Suppose the exam combines both:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why are encoding and scaling needed before ML?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A good answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Encoding is needed because many machine-learning algorithms require mathematical numerical representations rather than raw textual categories. However, directly assigning integers to unordered categories can introduce false relationships, for example suggesting that Green = 2 is greater than Red = 0. One-hot encoding avoids this by creating separate binary indicators for each category. Scaling addresses a different problem: numerical variables may have widely different ranges, such as Age and Income. In distance-based algorithms, the larger-valued feature can dominate calculations solely because of its units. Scaling therefore places numerical features on more comparable mathematical footing. Thus, encoding addresses how categories are represented, while scaling addresses differences in numerical magnitude.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That last sentence is excellent for \"compare\" questions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P11</span><span>The Lecture 2 answer framework to use in the exam</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Whenever you are given a dataset scenario, mentally run:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Problem → Data Type → Data Quality → Transformation → Consistency → Evaluation\"><span>Problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Type</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Quality</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transformation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Consistency</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluation</span></div>\n<p>Ask yourself:</p>\n<p><strong>What is the business objective?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>What types of variables do I have?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>What's wrong with the data?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>What preprocessing technique solves that specific problem?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>Why is that technique appropriate?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>Am I accidentally using test information during preprocessing?</strong></p>\n<p>If you answer subjective questions with that logic rather than throwing around words like <code>fillna</code>, \"scaling\" and \"one-hot encoding,\" you'll demonstrate that you actually understand the pipeline rather than merely remembering pandas commands.</p></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"l2s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">50 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 2 - Preparing for a Subjective Exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s0\" title=\"Lecture 2 - Preparing for a Subjective Exam\">Overview</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 1 - How to answer Lecture 2 subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s1\" title=\"Part 1 - How to answer Lecture 2 subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Answer structure</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - CRISP-DM: the project-level framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s2\" title=\"Part 2 - CRISP-DM: the project-level framework\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>CRISP-DM phases</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - Business Understanding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s3\" title=\"Part 3 - Business Understanding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Business Understanding</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - Data Understanding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s4\" title=\"Part 4 - Data Understanding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Data Understanding</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - Data Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s5\" title=\"Part 5 - Data Preparation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Data Preparation</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Modeling, Evaluation and Deployment\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s6\" title=\"Part 6 - Modeling, Evaluation and Deployment\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Modeling, evaluation, deployment</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - CRISP-DM vs Machine Learning Pipeline\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s7\" title=\"Part 7 - CRISP-DM vs Machine Learning Pipeline\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>CRISP-DM vs ML pipeline</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - The most important technical rule: preprocessing consistency\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s8\" title=\"Part 8 - The most important technical rule: preprocessing consistency\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Preprocessing consistency</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - Data leakage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s9\" title=\"Part 9 - Data leakage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Data leakage</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - Data sources and formats\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s10\" title=\"Part 10 - Data sources and formats\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Data sources and formats</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - Data types determine preprocessing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s11\" title=\"Part 11 - Data types determine preprocessing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Data types</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - Data wrangling\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s12\" title=\"Part 12 - Data wrangling\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Data wrangling</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - Inspect before cleaning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s13\" title=\"Part 13 - Inspect before cleaning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Inspect before cleaning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - `info()` vs `describe()`\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s14\" title=\"Part 14 - info() vs describe()\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>info() vs describe()</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 - Missing values: don&#x27;t blindly delete everything\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s15\" title=\"Part 15 - Missing values: don&#x27;t blindly delete everything\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Missing values</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 - Strategy 1: Remove\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s16\" title=\"Part 16 - Strategy 1: Remove\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Drop missing rows</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 17 - Strategy 2: Imputation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s17\" title=\"Part 17 - Strategy 2: Imputation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Imputation</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 18 - Why mean vs median matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s18\" title=\"Part 18 - Why mean vs median matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Mean vs median</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 19 - Strategy 3: Forward and backward fill\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s19\" title=\"Part 19 - Strategy 3: Forward and backward fill\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Forward / backward fill</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 20 - How to answer a missing-data scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s20\" title=\"Part 20 - How to answer a missing-data scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Model answer: missing data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 21 - Duplicates\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s21\" title=\"Part 21 - Duplicates\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Duplicates</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 22 - Why duplicates can be dangerous\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s22\" title=\"Part 22 - Why duplicates can be dangerous\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Why duplicates hurt</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 23 - Data-type conversion\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s23\" title=\"Part 23 - Data-type conversion\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Type conversion</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 24 - Feature engineering in Lecture 2\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s24\" title=\"Part 24 - Feature engineering in Lecture 2\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Feature engineering</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 25 - Categorical variables\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s25\" title=\"Part 25 - Categorical variables\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Categorical variables</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 26 - Label encoding vs One-Hot Encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s26\" title=\"Part 26 - Label encoding vs One-Hot Encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Label vs one-hot</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 27 - Likely subjective encoding question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s27\" title=\"Part 27 - Likely subjective encoding question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Model answer: encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 28 - Feature scaling\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s28\" title=\"Part 28 - Feature scaling\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Feature scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 29 - Why scaling matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s29\" title=\"Part 29 - Why scaling matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Why scaling matters</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 30 - Min-Max vs Standardization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s30\" title=\"Part 30 - Min-Max vs Standardization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Min-max vs z-score</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 31 - A strong scaling answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s31\" title=\"Part 31 - A strong scaling answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Model answer: scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 32 - Basic text preprocessing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s32\" title=\"Part 32 - Basic text preprocessing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Text preprocessing</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 33 - Data summarization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s33\" title=\"Part 33 - Data summarization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Summarizing data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 34 - Understand `groupby()` as Split-Apply-Combine\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s34\" title=\"Part 34 - Understand groupby() as Split-Apply-Combine\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>groupby: split-apply-combine</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 35 - Likely `groupby()` subjective question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s35\" title=\"Part 35 - Likely groupby() subjective question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Model answer: groupby</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 36 - Visualization is part of understanding data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s36\" title=\"Part 36 - Visualization is part of understanding data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Why visualize</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 37 - How to justify chart selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s37\" title=\"Part 37 - How to justify chart selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Choosing a chart</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 38 - Histogram vs Bar Chart\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s38\" title=\"Part 38 - Histogram vs Bar Chart\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Histogram vs bar chart</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 39 - Box Plot\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s39\" title=\"Part 39 - Box Plot\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Box plot</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 40 - Putting the whole pipeline together\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s40\" title=\"Part 40 - Putting the whole pipeline together\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Whole pipeline in order</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 41 - Full scenario example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s41\" title=\"Part 41 - Full scenario example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>Scenario: telecom customers</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 42 - Very likely question: explain preprocessing consistency and leakage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s42\" title=\"Part 42 - Very likely question: explain preprocessing consistency and leakage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>Likely Q: consistency and leakage</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 43 - Very likely question: explain why data preparation matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s43\" title=\"Part 43 - Very likely question: explain why data preparation matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Likely Q: why data prep matters</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 44 - Very likely question: missing data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s44\" title=\"Part 44 - Very likely question: missing data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>Likely Q: missing data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 45 - Very likely question: categorical encoding and scaling\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s45\" title=\"Part 45 - Very likely question: categorical encoding and scaling\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>Likely Q: encoding and scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 46 - How to interpret short code in a subjective paper\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s46\" title=\"Part 46 - How to interpret short code in a subjective paper\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>Explaining short code</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 47 - The distinctions I would drill hardest\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s47\" title=\"Part 47 - The distinctions I would drill hardest\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>Key distinctions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 48 - How Lecture 2 connects to Lecture 1\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s48\" title=\"Part 48 - How Lecture 2 connects to Lecture 1\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>Link to Lecture 1</button></li><li data-full=\"The Lecture 2 answer framework to use in the exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s49\" title=\"The Lecture 2 answer framework to use in the exam\">Exam answer framework</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Lecture 2 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>50 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 2 - Preparing for a Subjective Exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Machine Learning Pipeline: From Raw Data to a Model</h4>\n<p>Lecture 1 asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What is machine learning?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Lecture 2 asks the much more practical question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do we actually build a machine-learning solution from messy real-world data?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The central idea of the lecture is that <strong>the algorithm is only one part of the job</strong>. You should not receive a problem and immediately say \"Random Forest\" or \"neural network.\" The workflow starts with understanding the problem, obtaining and understanding the data, cleaning and transforming it, and only then modelling and evaluating. The lecture's learning outcomes therefore emphasize CRISP-DM, data formats, inspection, cleaning, encoding, scaling, transformation, summarization and visualization.</p>\n<p>For a subjective exam, I would think of Lecture 2 as one continuous story:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Business Problem → Data → Inspect → Clean → Transform → Model → Evaluate → Deploy\"><span>Business Problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Inspect</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clean</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transform</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deploy</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - How to answer Lecture 2 subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For most scenario questions, use this reasoning pattern:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Identify Problem → Identify Data Issue → Choose Technique → Explain Why → Explain Consequence\"><span>Identify Problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Identify Data Issue</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Choose Technique</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Explain Why</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Explain Consequence</span></div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A customer dataset contains some missing income values and several extreme salaries. Recommend an appropriate way to handle the missing income.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Use median.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A stronger answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The missing income values can be imputed rather than deleting the entire observations, particularly if retaining the rows is important. Since the salary distribution contains extreme values, the median is preferable to the mean because the median is less influenced by outliers. This provides a more representative replacement value while preserving the remaining information in those customer records.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture itself distinguishes mean imputation for roughly normal numerical data from median imputation when outliers are present.</p>\n<p>Notice the pattern:</p>\n<p><strong>choice + reason + effect.</strong></p>\n<p>That is what subjective marking rewards.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - CRISP-DM: the project-level framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The first major concept is <strong>CRISP-DM</strong>, or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Cross}\\text{-}\\text{Industry Standard Process for Data Mining}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Its six phases are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Business Understanding → Data Understanding → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment\"><span>Business Understanding</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Understanding</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Preparation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Modeling</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deployment</span></div>\n<p>Do not memorize these as six random words. Each stage answers a different question.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - Business Understanding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The first question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What problem are we actually trying to solve?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose a factory says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We have too much unexpected downtime.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is a business problem.</p>\n<p>You need to translate it into something machine learning can solve:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Predict whether a machine will fail within the next 24 hours.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now you have something specific.</p>\n<p>The lecture's example explicitly converts:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Reduce unexpected downtime}$$</div>\n<p>into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Predict equipment failure within 24h}$$</div>\n<p>and asks what outcome is desired and how success will be measured.</p>\n<p>A strong subjective sentence is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Business Understanding ensures that the ML system solves a meaningful organizational problem rather than merely producing technically interesting predictions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - Data Understanding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Once the problem is defined, ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What information do we actually have?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For machine failure, perhaps:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">temperature\npressure\nvibration\nmachine age\nmaintenance logs\nfailure history\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then ask:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Is it complete?\nIs it relevant?\nAre there missing values?\nAre there obvious patterns?\nAre there quality problems?\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture places these questions directly under Data Understanding.</p>\n<p>This matters because a model can only learn from information that actually exists.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - Data Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we start fixing the data.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes this phase as including:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cleaning\ntransforming\nmissing-value handling\nencoding\nscaling\n</code></pre>\n<p>and calls it the <strong>heaviest stage</strong>.</p>\n<p>This should immediately tell you something important:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In real machine learning, model training is not necessarily the largest part of the work.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Most of Lecture 2 is really zooming into this phase.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Modeling, Evaluation and Deployment</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h5>Modeling</h5>\n<p>Choose suitable algorithms, train them and tune their parameters.</p>\n<h5>Evaluation</h5>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does this model actually satisfy the criteria we defined during Business Understanding?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture specifically says that if it does not meet the Phase 1 criteria, you <strong>iterate</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is important.</p>\n<p>A model with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$95\\% \\ \\text{accuracy}$$</div>\n<p>is not necessarily useful if the business requirement was something that the model does poorly.</p>\n<h5>Deployment</h5>\n<p>Integrate the final model into a live system so that it can generate real predictions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - CRISP-DM vs Machine Learning Pipeline</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_crisp_vs_pipeline\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Not two competing processes: the ML pipeline runs inside CRISP-DM&#x27;s preparation, modelling and evaluation phases.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This distinction is very likely subjective material.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly says:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{CRISP}\\text{-}\\text{DM} = \\text{Project Framework}}$$</div>\n<p>while:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{ML Pipeline} = \\text{Technical Workflow}}$$</div>\n<p>Think of it this way.</p>\n<p>CRISP-DM asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why are we building this?<br />\nWhat business outcome matters?<br />\nWhat data do we have?<br />\nDoes the final result solve the original problem?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The ML pipeline asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What technical steps transform incoming data into a usable prediction?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The standard ML pipeline mainly contains:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Data Retrieval → Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment\"><span>Data Retrieval</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Preparation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Modeling</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deployment</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Likely subjective question</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare CRISP-DM and a Machine Learning Pipeline.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>CRISP-DM is a broad project framework that guides a data-science project from business objectives through data understanding, preparation, modelling, evaluation and deployment. It considers strategy and the needs of stakeholders in addition to technical work. In contrast, the machine-learning pipeline is the more technical workflow used to process data and produce predictions, including data retrieval, preprocessing, feature preparation, model training, evaluation and deployment. Therefore, CRISP-DM explains the overall project lifecycle, while the ML pipeline operationalizes the technical parts of that lifecycle.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's much stronger than:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>CRISP-DM is business, pipeline is technical.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - The most important technical rule: preprocessing consistency</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_consistency\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Parameters are learned once from training data and reused unchanged for every new record.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This concept is extremely important.</p>\n<p>During training:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Training Data → Preprocessing → Model Training\"><span>Training Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Preprocessing</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model Training</span></div>\n<p>During deployment:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{New Data}\n\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{SAME PREPROCESSING}}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Prediction}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture explicitly emphasizes this rule.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because the model learned patterns based on a particular representation.</p>\n<p>Imagine training income was scaled using:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">minimum = RM2,000\nmaximum = RM20,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>The model learned from those transformed values.</p>\n<p>When a new customer arrives, you should use those same learned parameters.</p>\n<p>You should not invent a brand-new scaling system for each new customer.</p>\n<p>Otherwise:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Training Representation}\n\\neq\n\\text{Deployment Representation}$$</div>\n<p>and the model receives values that no longer mean what it was trained to interpret.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - Data leakage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_leakage\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Data leakage in scaling</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Fit the scaler on training data only, or on everything - and see what leaks.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This preprocessing rule connects directly to <strong>data leakage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines preprocessing leakage as using information from the test set, such as recalculating scaling minimum/maximum values using test data, which can artificially inflate performance.</p>\n<p>Imagine an exam.</p>\n<p>You have:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Study questions\nFinal exam questions\n</code></pre>\n<p>You promise not to see the final exam.</p>\n<p>But before studying, you calculate:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">most common final-exam topic\naverage difficulty\nimportant words in final questions\n</code></pre>\n<p>You've already extracted information from the exam.</p>\n<p>That's leakage.</p>\n<p>The same idea applies in ML.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Correct workflow</h4>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">scaler.fit(X_train)\n\nX_train_scaled = scaler.transform(X_train)\nX_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The critical idea isn't Python syntax.</p>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Learn preprocessing from training data}\n}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Reuse that preprocessing on test/new data}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Not:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">scaler.fit(X_train)\nscaler.fit(X_test)   # conceptually wrong\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - Data sources and formats</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_json_flatten\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>JSON arrives as a tree; ML wants rows and columns, so nested lists become extra rows.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Before cleaning, you need data.</p>\n<p>The lecture discusses several common forms.</p>\n<h5>CSV</h5>\n<p>Naturally tabular:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Name,Age,Score\nAli,20,85\nSara,21,91\n</code></pre>\n<p>Very convenient for pandas.</p>\n<h5>JSON</h5>\n<p>Usually hierarchical key-value structures:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ali&quot;,\n  &quot;age&quot;: 20\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Because it may be nested, it can require flattening before classical ML.</p>\n<h5>XML</h5>\n<p>A tag-based hierarchical format, generally more verbose.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly contrasts CSV as naturally tabular with JSON as hierarchical and XML as a more verbose structure requiring appropriate parsing.</p>\n<h5>SQL</h5>\n<p>Used to retrieve information stored in relational databases.</p>\n<p>The exam probably won't care whether you adore XML. Nobody does. The important thing is to understand that <strong>data sources have different structures and therefore may require different preparation before they become an ML-ready table</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - Data types determine preprocessing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is an important reasoning principle:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Different Data Type}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{Different Preprocessing}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture gives:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Typical treatment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Numeric</td>\n<td>Age, Price</td>\n<td>Scaling/normalization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Categorical</td>\n<td>Colour, Size</td>\n<td>Encoding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>Reviews</td>\n<td>Tokenization etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Datetime</td>\n<td>Timestamp</td>\n<td>Extract day/month/duration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>So in a scenario, before doing anything, classify your variables.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - Data wrangling</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture defines <strong>data wrangling</strong> as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>cleaning, transforming, restructuring and preparing raw data so it becomes suitable for analysis and machine learning.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Mental picture:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Messy Raw Data → Wrangling → ML-Ready Data\"><span>Messy Raw Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Wrangling</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>ML-Ready Data</span></div>\n<p>And the slide immediately gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Garbage In} \\rightarrow \\text{Garbage Out}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The point is simple:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A clever algorithm cannot magically rescue fundamentally bad input data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - Inspect before cleaning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you receive:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Product</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Quantity</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2023-01-15</td>\n<td>P101</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2023-01-15</td>\n<td>P105</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">NaN</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">85.50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16/01/2023</td>\n<td>P101</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">150</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Before changing anything, <strong>inspect it</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.info()\ndf.describe()\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>df.info()</code> helps reveal:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">row/column information\ndata types\nnon-null counts\n</code></pre>\n<p>while <code>df.describe()</code> gives statistical summaries of numerical columns.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - <code>info()</code> vs <code>describe()</code></span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is easy to turn into a subjective coding question.</p>\n<h5><code>df.info()</code></h5>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Structure</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Useful for:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">data types\nmissingness through non-null counts\ngeneral dataframe information\n</code></pre>\n<h5><code>df.describe()</code></h5>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Statistics</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Useful for:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">mean\nstandard deviation\nminimum\nmaximum\n</code></pre>\n<p>A good answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>info()</code> is primarily used to understand the structure and completeness of the dataset, whereas <code>describe()</code> summarizes the statistical behaviour of numerical variables and can help reveal unusual ranges or potential outliers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 - Missing values: don't blindly delete everything</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Missing data is not one problem with one universal solution.</p>\n<p>The lecture presents three broad strategies:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Remove}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Impute}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Forward/Backward Fill}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 - Strategy 1: Remove</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.dropna()\n</code></pre>\n<p>This removes records with missing values.</p>\n<p>The lecture suggests this can be suitable when the dataset is large and missing values are very limited, for example below about 1%.</p>\n<p>But think about the consequence.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.5\\%$$</div>\n<p>of rows are missing, deletion may be acceptable.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$40\\%$$</div>\n<p>are missing, deleting everything affected would massacre the dataset.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically warns that blindly deleting missing rows can destroy too much data.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 17 - Strategy 2: Imputation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of deleting:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Replace the missing value.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;price&quot;].fillna(mean)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture recommends:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Mean}$$</div>\n<p>for suitable numerical data with a normal-type distribution, and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Median}$$</div>\n<p>when outliers are present.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 18 - Why mean vs median matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_impute\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Mean versus median imputation</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Change the outlier and see why the median is the safer fill value.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Consider:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100,\\ 110,\\ 120,\\ 130,\\ 1000$$</div>\n<p>The value:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1000$$</div>\n<p>pulls the mean upward substantially.</p>\n<p>The median remains closer to the typical observation.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When extreme observations exist, median imputation can represent the centre more robustly than mean imputation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's the reasoning the examiner wants.</p>\n<p>Don't merely write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Outliers → median.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Explain why.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 19 - Strategy 3: Forward and backward fill</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose time-series data looks like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">10:00 → 20\n10:01 → missing\n10:02 → 21\n</code></pre>\n<p>If the previous state tends to persist, you may use:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.ffill()\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture specifically links forward filling to time-series situations where the previous state usually persists.</p>\n<p>Forward fill:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{previous known value}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{missing position}$$</div>\n<p>Backward fill uses the following known value instead.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 20 - How to answer a missing-data scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A temperature sensor records a value every minute. One minute is missing, but temperature usually changes gradually. Recommend a method.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Forward filling may be appropriate because this is sequential time-series data and the immediately previous sensor state is likely to remain reasonably representative for a short missing interval. This preserves the record without deleting the observation. However, this approach relies on the assumption that the underlying state changes gradually; it would be less appropriate if measurements can change abruptly.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That last sentence adds evaluation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 21 - Duplicates</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture gives an excellent warning:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Do not automatically delete every duplicate-looking record.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Coffee RM8.50 at 9:00\nCoffee RM8.50 at 9:00\n</code></pre>\n<p>That could be:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">same transaction accidentally duplicated\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">two genuine coffees purchased\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly uses this reasoning.</p>\n<p>So context matters.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 22 - Why duplicates can be dangerous</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Duplicates can:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">distort counts\npull averages toward repeated values\ncause leakage if copies appear in train and test\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly identifies all of these risks.</p>\n<p>Core code:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.duplicated().sum()\ndf.drop_duplicates(inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>But again:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>identify first, understand second, remove third.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't delete first and ask questions later.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 23 - Data-type conversion</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Humans may look at:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;2026-10-05&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>and think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Date.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The computer may see:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">string\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you need date operations:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;date&quot;] = pd.to_datetime(df[&quot;date&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>Likewise:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;150.00&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>may look numeric to us but still be stored as text.</p>\n<p>Then conversion to numeric is necessary.</p>\n<p>The larger subjective point is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A value's appearance and its computational data type are not necessarily the same.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 24 - Feature engineering in Lecture 2</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Feature engineering here means deriving more useful information from existing data.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">date = 2026-10-05\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can derive:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.month\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;is_weekend&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.dayofweek &gt; 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">month\nweekend\n</code></pre>\n<p>may expose patterns that the raw timestamp does not make as obvious to the model.</p>\n<p>This is the conceptual transition into Lectures 3-4.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 25 - Categorical variables</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>ML algorithms operate mathematically, so categorical values such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red\nBlue\nGreen\n</code></pre>\n<p>often need numerical representation.</p>\n<p>But this creates an immediate danger.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red   = 0\nBlue  = 1\nGreen = 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>A model may interpret:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Green} &gt; \\text{Blue} &gt; \\text{Red}$$</div>\n<p>even though colour has no meaningful magnitude.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly warns about this false-order problem.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 26 - Label encoding vs One-Hot Encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l3_encoder\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Categorical encodings side by side</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type your own categories and compare label, one-hot, dummy, effect and hashing encodings.</figcaption></figure></div><h5>Label encoding</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Red}\\rightarrow0$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Blue}\\rightarrow1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Green}\\rightarrow2$$</div>\n<p>Compact, but may imply a false order.</p>\n<h5>One-hot encoding</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Red}\\rightarrow[1,0,0]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Blue}\\rightarrow[0,1,0]$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Green}\\rightarrow[0,0,1]$$</div>\n<p>Each category becomes its own binary indicator.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>One-hot encoding is useful when categories have no meaningful numerical order because it avoids imposing an artificial magnitude relationship.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's the subjective reasoning to remember.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 27 - Likely subjective encoding question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>Explain why one-hot encoding may be preferable to directly assigning integers to a categorical variable such as colour.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Directly assigning integers such as Red = 0, Blue = 1 and Green = 2 converts the categories into numbers but may cause an algorithm to interpret an artificial ordering or magnitude between them. One-hot encoding instead represents each category using its own binary feature, such as Red = [1,0,0], so no category is treated as numerically greater than another. Therefore, one-hot encoding better preserves the unordered nature of such categorical data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 28 - Feature scaling</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a dataset contains:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Age}=25$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Income}=45000$$</div>\n<p>Another customer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Age}=30$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Income}=55000$$</div>\n<p>Difference in age:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5$$</div>\n<p>Difference in income:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10000$$</div>\n<p>For a distance-based algorithm, income can dominate simply because its raw numerical scale is much larger.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically uses Age and Income to demonstrate this problem for algorithms such as KNN and SVM.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 29 - Why scaling matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Scaling does <strong>not</strong> mean:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Income and age are equally important.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Don't let arbitrary units decide their mathematical importance.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Income measured in RM\n</code></pre>\n<p>has large numbers.</p>\n<p>If I changed units to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">thousands of RM\n</code></pre>\n<p>the values suddenly become much smaller.</p>\n<p>The meaning didn't change.</p>\n<p>A model shouldn't behave radically differently merely because I changed measurement units.</p>\n<p>That's the intuition behind scaling.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 30 - Min-Max vs Standardization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_scaling\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Why feature scaling matters</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · How much of the distance between two customers comes from age versus income, before and after scaling.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives two common approaches.</p>\n<h5>Min-Max Scaling</h5>\n<p>Compress values roughly into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0\\rightarrow1$$</div>\n<h5>Standardization / Z-score</h5>\n<p>Transform the distribution so that it is centered around:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Mean}=0$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{StandardDeviation}=1$$</div>\n<p>For a subjective question, the main issue isn't memorizing formulas. Explain the purpose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Both reduce problems caused by features having very different numerical scales, but they use different transformations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 31 - A strong scaling answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>Feature scaling is particularly important for algorithms whose calculations depend on numerical distance. For example, if Age ranges from 20 to 70 while Income ranges from RM20,000 to RM500,000, Income may dominate the distance calculation simply due to its larger numerical magnitude. Scaling places features on more comparable mathematical scales. The lecture introduces Min-Max scaling, which compresses values between 0 and 1, and standardization, which centers a variable around mean 0 with standard deviation 1.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's a solid 5-mark response.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 32 - Basic text preprocessing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_text_pipeline\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Text preprocessing pipeline</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type any sentence and watch each preprocessing step.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Lecture 2 introduces text only at a basic level.</p>\n<p>For:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;The cats are running!&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>we may eventually produce something like:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[&quot;cat&quot;, &quot;run&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture introduces three main ideas:</p>\n<p><strong>Tokenization</strong> - split text into units/words.</p>\n<p><strong>Stopword removal</strong> - remove common low-information terms.</p>\n<p><strong>Stemming/Lemmatization</strong> - reduce words toward root or canonical forms.</p>\n<p>Don't turn a Lecture 2 answer into a dissertation on transformers and BERT. That's Lecture 2 being asked to carry groceries it never bought.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 33 - Data summarization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you have one million transaction rows.</p>\n<p>Reading:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">row 1\nrow 2\nrow 3\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>is useless.</p>\n<p>You need summaries.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<p>which asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What is the average price for each user type?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 34 - Understand <code>groupby()</code> as Split-Apply-Combine</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_groupby\"><div class=\"vis-title\">groupby step by step</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Step through split → apply → combine.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is the cleanest way to explain it:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Split → Apply → Combine\"><span>Split</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Apply</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Combine</span></div>\n<p>The lecture defines it explicitly this way.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>User Type</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">700</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5>Split</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">New → 100, 200\nPremium → 500, 700\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Apply mean</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{New}=\\frac{100+200}{2}=150$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Premium}=\\frac{500+700}{2}=600$$</div>\n<h5>Combine</h5>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>User Type</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Mean Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">600</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That's what <code>groupby()</code> is conceptually doing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 35 - Likely <code>groupby()</code> subjective question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>Explain the output of:</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Good answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The statement divides the transaction records according to <code>user_type</code>, calculates the mean value of <code>price</code> separately for each user group, and combines those averages into a summarized result. This follows the split-apply-combine process: split records by user category, apply the mean operation, and combine the group-level results.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Much better than:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It groups data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 36 - Visualization is part of understanding data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture's advice is effectively:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Understand the data before training the model.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Charts are not there just because reports look lonely without them.</p>\n<p>Different visualizations answer different questions.</p>\n<p>The lecture provides this mapping:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Appropriate chart</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trend over time</td>\n<td>Line chart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare numerical values across categories</td>\n<td>Bar chart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution of one numerical variable</td>\n<td>Histogram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spread and outliers</td>\n<td>Box plot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationship between two numerical variables</td>\n<td>Scatter plot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 37 - How to justify chart selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_chart_picker\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Choosing a chart</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Pick the question; the right chart follows.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Recommend a visualization to investigate the relationship between Income and Spending.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't simply write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Scatter plot.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A scatter plot is appropriate because both Income and Spending are numerical variables, and each observation can be plotted as a point using one variable on each axis. This allows the analyst to inspect whether increases in income are associated with increases or decreases in spending and to identify unusual observations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The justification is where the marks usually live.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 38 - Histogram vs Bar Chart</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A classic confusion.</p>\n<h5>Histogram</h5>\n<p>Used for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Distribution of one numerical variable}$$</div>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">customer ages\nsalary values\ntransaction amounts\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Bar chart</h5>\n<p>Used to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Compare categories}$$</div>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Sales by branch\nCustomers by plan type\n</code></pre>\n<p>The bars can look superficially similar, but the questions they answer are different.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 39 - Box Plot</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Use when you care about:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">spread\ncentral distribution\nextreme observations / potential outliers\n</code></pre>\n<p>So if asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Before choosing mean or median salary imputation, what visualization may help identify extreme salaries?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A <strong>box plot</strong> is a sensible answer because the lecture explicitly associates it with spread and outliers.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 40 - Putting the whole pipeline together</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture's final customer example essentially follows:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Business Goal}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Collect Data}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Inspect and Clean}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Handle Missing Values}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Encode Categories}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Scale Features}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Train}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Evaluate}$$</div>\n<p>This is how I would reconstruct Lecture 2 if you go blank in the exam.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 41 - Full scenario example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the subjective question says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A telecom company wants to predict customer purchasing behaviour. Its dataset contains Age, Income, Plan Type and Sign-Up Date. Some Income values are missing, Plan Type is text, and Sign-Up Date has been loaded as a string. Explain how you would prepare the data before modelling.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Reason through it sequentially.</p>\n<h5>First: inspect</h5>\n<p>Use tools such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.info()\ndf.describe()\n</code></pre>\n<p>to identify:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">types\nmissingness\nnumerical ranges\npossible unusual observations\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Second: Income</h5>\n<p>Investigate the distribution and missingness.</p>\n<p>If only a few rows are missing and the dataset is huge, removal may be acceptable.</p>\n<p>Otherwise impute.</p>\n<p>If there are serious outliers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Median}$$</div>\n<p>may be preferable.</p>\n<h5>Third: Plan Type</h5>\n<p>It's categorical.</p>\n<p>If categories have no meaningful numeric ordering, encode them in a way that avoids creating false magnitude, such as one-hot representation.</p>\n<h5>Fourth: Sign-Up Date</h5>\n<p>Convert it to a proper datetime type, then relevant components can be extracted if needed.</p>\n<h5>Fifth: Scaling</h5>\n<p>If the selected model is sensitive to feature magnitudes, scale numerical variables such as Age and Income using parameters learned from the training data.</p>\n<h5>Sixth: avoid leakage</h5>\n<p>Do not use test-set information to fit the scaler or other preprocessing transformations.</p>\n<p>That is a very respectable subjective answer because you've shown a <strong>workflow</strong>, not thrown disconnected vocabulary at the examiner.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 42 - Very likely question: explain preprocessing consistency and leakage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A good 8-10 mark answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Preprocessing consistency means that new or test observations must undergo the same transformations used during model training. For example, if numerical scaling parameters or categorical mappings are learned from the training data, those same parameters should be applied to test and deployment data rather than recalculated independently. This ensures that the model receives features represented in the same way as during training. It also prevents data leakage. Leakage occurs when information from the test set influences model development or preprocessing, such as using test values when calculating scaling minimums and maximums. This makes evaluation unreliable because the supposedly unseen test data has already influenced the model-building process. Therefore, preprocessing rules should be learned from training data and then reused unchanged for test and future observations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I would learn that paragraph's <strong>logic</strong>, not memorize every word.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 43 - Very likely question: explain why data preparation matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Model answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Data preparation is necessary because real-world datasets may contain missing values, duplicates, inconsistent formats, incorrect data types, categorical text and features measured on very different scales. These issues can prevent algorithms from operating correctly or can distort the patterns that the model learns. Data preparation therefore involves inspecting the dataset, cleaning errors, handling missing information, converting data types, encoding categories, scaling relevant numerical features and deriving useful features. The purpose is to transform messy raw data into a reliable and consistent representation suitable for modelling. As emphasized in the lecture, algorithms cannot compensate for fundamentally poor input data; therefore, data preparation directly affects downstream model quality.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 44 - Very likely question: missing data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Discuss methods for handling missing data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Your answer should compare, not merely list.</p>\n<p>You could write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Missing values can be handled through deletion or imputation depending on the amount and nature of the missing data. <code>dropna()</code> removes affected records and may be reasonable when the dataset is large and only a very small proportion of values is missing. However, excessive deletion can unnecessarily reduce the dataset. Alternatively, numerical values can be imputed using a central value; the lecture recommends mean for suitable normally distributed data and median where outliers are important. For ordered or time-series data, forward or backward filling can also be used when neighbouring observations provide a reasonable estimate, such as when the previous state tends to persist. Therefore, the method should be selected according to the data's distribution and structure rather than applying one technique blindly.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's the level of comparison you want.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 45 - Very likely question: categorical encoding and scaling</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the exam combines both:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why are encoding and scaling needed before ML?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A good answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Encoding is needed because many machine-learning algorithms require mathematical numerical representations rather than raw textual categories. However, directly assigning integers to unordered categories can introduce false relationships, for example suggesting that Green = 2 is greater than Red = 0. One-hot encoding avoids this by creating separate binary indicators for each category. Scaling addresses a different problem: numerical variables may have widely different ranges, such as Age and Income. In distance-based algorithms, the larger-valued feature can dominate calculations solely because of its units. Scaling therefore places numerical features on more comparable mathematical footing. Thus, encoding addresses how categories are represented, while scaling addresses differences in numerical magnitude.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That last sentence is excellent for \"compare\" questions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 46 - How to interpret short code in a subjective paper</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You probably won't be asked to build an entire pipeline from memory. More likely, you'll see code and be asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What does this do and why?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>These are the lines I would understand cold:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.info()\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Inspect dataframe structure, data types and non-null counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.describe()\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Summarize numerical statistics.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.dropna()\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Remove records containing missing values.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;price&quot;].fillna(df[&quot;price&quot;].median())\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Replace missing prices using the median.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.duplicated().sum()\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Count duplicate rows.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.drop_duplicates()\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Remove duplicate records.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;date&quot;] = pd.to_datetime(df[&quot;date&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Convert the column to datetime representation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[&quot;plan&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Create binary indicators for categories.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Calculate average price separately for each user type.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The important part in a subjective exam is explaining <strong>why the operation might be needed</strong>, not merely translating Python into English.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 47 - The distinctions I would drill hardest</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For Lecture 2, I would make sure you can confidently explain these five pairs:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pair</th>\n<th>Core distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline</td>\n<td>Project framework vs technical workflow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>info()</code> vs <code>describe()</code></td>\n<td>Structure/types vs statistical summary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dropna()</code> vs <code>fillna()</code></td>\n<td>Remove missing observations vs replace missing values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Label vs One-Hot Encoding</td>\n<td>Integer representation vs separate binary categories</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min-Max vs Standardization</td>\n<td>0-1 range vs mean 0 / SD 1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Then understand this rule better than anything else:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Fit preprocessing on training data}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Reuse on test/new data}\n}$$</div>\n<p>because it connects preprocessing consistency, deployment consistency and data leakage.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s48\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 48 - How Lecture 2 connects to Lecture 1</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Lecture 1 taught you:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Task} + \\text{Experience} + \\text{Performance}$$</div>\n<p>Lecture 2 now tells you:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we actually obtain trustworthy experience $E$?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If the data is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">missing\nduplicated\nincorrectly typed\npoorly encoded\nbadly scaled\n</code></pre>\n<p>then the experience supplied to the model is poor.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Poor Data → Poor Learning → Poor Performance\"><span>Poor Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Poor Learning</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Poor Performance</span></div>\n<p>This is why Lecture 2 concludes with essentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Good ML} =\n\\text{Good Data}\n+\n\\text{Solid Preparation}\n+\n\\text{Proper Evaluation}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The final assessment slide reinforces exactly that idea.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The Lecture 2 answer framework to use in the exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Whenever you are given a dataset scenario, mentally run:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Problem → Data Type → Data Quality → Transformation → Consistency → Evaluation\"><span>Problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Type</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Quality</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transformation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Consistency</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluation</span></div>\n<p>Ask yourself:</p>\n<p><strong>What is the business objective?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>What types of variables do I have?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>What's wrong with the data?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>What preprocessing technique solves that specific problem?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>Why is that technique appropriate?</strong></p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>Am I accidentally using test information during preprocessing?</strong></p>\n<p>If you answer subjective questions with that logic rather than throwing around words like <code>fillna</code>, \"scaling\" and \"one-hot encoding,\" you'll demonstrate that you actually understand the pipeline rather than merely remembering pandas commands.</p></div></details></div></details>");
